\expand replaces the names inside its curly brackets with
their contents, one level deeper than calling a name as an
argument does. It is used as an argument to other commands.
Its syntax is the following:

\expand {\name}

A loop name as an argument to a command gives the names of
its bars, separated by |. With \expand, these names are
replaced by the bars themselves. For example, if \myloop
holds the bars \bar1 and \bar2, the line below prints the
notes of \inst1 in both bars, separated by |:

\print \expand {\myloop.\inst1}

The | separators are kept as they are. A variable that
holds the name of a bar or loop can also be expanded this
way.

\expand takes at least one argument, otherwise it throws
an error.
